WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.663
Think about a hairdresser. Almost all the work is done by a skilled person, with just scissors and a hairdryer. Now think about a car factory, where robots weld and paint the cars.

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:25.348
In production, capital means machines, equipment and technology. Labour means the people who do the work. Careful: here, capital does not mean money.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:37.594
Capital-intensive production uses more machinery than labour, like a car plant full of robots. Labour-intensive production uses more workers than machinery, like a hairdresser, a chef or a fruit picker.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:51.971
Step one. Ask a single question: who or what does most of the work here, machines or people? Machines means capital intensive. People means labour intensive.

00:00:52.550 --> 00:01:02.446
Step two. For a Define question, write one sentence that compares the two inputs. Say which one is used more than the other.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:20.608
Step three. In multiple choice, rule out the distractors. Kaizen and just-in-time are lean production methods. They are not about intensity. Also remember: job production is usually labour intensive, and flow production is usually capital intensive.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:32.853
Here is a real exam question from Paper two in November 2023. Define the term labour intensive production. It is worth one mark.

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:41.485
First, find the input it relies on. Labour means workers, so labour-intensive production relies mainly on people.

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:55.651
Then add the comparison. Labour-intensive production uses more workers than machinery and equipment. That one sentence earns the mark. This explanation is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:06.532
The most common mistake is thinking capital intensive means needing a lot of money. Here, capital means machinery.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:15.442
Another mistake is swapping the terms. A hairdresser is labour intensive, not capital intensive, because a person does the skilled work.

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:35.244
Now you try. A bakery uses huge automatic ovens and a packing machine, with just three workers watching the line. Is it capital intensive or labour intensive? Then write a one-sentence definition of that term. Pause and decide.

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:44.477
Machines do most of the work, and only three people watch the line. So the bakery is capital intensive.

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:59.697
The definition: capital-intensive production uses more machinery than labour. Remember the method: ask who does most of the work, name the term, then compare the two inputs in one sentence. Now try the practice questions.
